nlseek(1)                        Newline Seeker                       nlseek(1)

NAME
       nlseek  -  newline  seeker; reads a nlcache v1 file format newline table
       and follows it, outputting the corresponding line in the source file.

SYNOPSIS
       nlseek OPTIONS SOURCE INPUT OUTPUT OFFSET [prefix]

       OPTIONS

       `OPTIONS` should be a string of ASCII characters that should have  their
       corresponding  flag  active.   Below is a comprehensive list of said op-
       tions.

       Verbosity
       q  - Quiet mode   suppress information
       Q  - QUIET mode   suppress warnings
       S  - SHUTUP mode  suppress errors
       Y  - Why mode     show debug messages
       Filing
       f  - Force overwrite if output file already exists.
       Output
       p  - Use text output instead of binary output.
       Miscellaneous
       v  - Show version then quit.
       h? - Show this help message then quit.

       Any other options will be ignored.  Note that non-ASCII  flags  may  set
       another flag that was not intended to be set.

       SOURCE

       Set  `SOURCE`  to  the file whereof the `INPUT` nlcache file is meant to
       represent.

       INPUT

       Set `INPUT` to the file whereof nlseek shall use as the  nlcache  table,
       or `-` for stdin.

       OUTPUT

       Set  `OUTPUT` to the file whereof nlseek shall use as the output, or `-`
       for stdout.

       OFFSET

       Set `OFFSET` to the line number thy seeks, starting from 1.

       [prefix]

       Optionally set `prefix` to the prefix that nlseek shall match  with  the
       line location.

DESCRIPTION
       nlseek  reads version 1 nlcache files produced by nlcache, and uses said
       nlcache file to locate the correct line swiftly.

EXIT STATUS
       nlseek outputs the following error codes depending on their cause.

       E#  Meaning
        0  nlseek succeeds in what it was asked to do
        1  nlseek failed because it does not know what the user wanted to do
        2  nlseek failed because it could not open the requested streams
        3  nlseek failed because the input nlcache file was malformed
        4  nlseek failed because table offset or prefix validation failed

NOTES
       nlseek will reject any command that does not have exactly  five  or  six
       parameters, even if help or version was requested.

       nlseek does not guarantee that it will not overwrite an preexisting file
       without force flag; nlseek only makes this event less likely by checking
       for existence first.

       nlseek  will  return  the requested line, if successful.  If a prefix is
       given, then if nlseek is successful, nlseek will provide the  characters
       after the prefix only.

       nlseek may be compiled with Limit2FIO32, which may make retrieval slower
       with  the  benefit  of compatibility.  To see if this is the case, check
       the version string for "Limit2FIO32".  If it shows "FIO64" instead, then
       Limit2FIO32 was not enabled.

